Congrats Rodrigo -- Looks very exciting!

Modifying the backing storage for a Renjin vector however may lead to quite some undefined behavior on Renjin's part however: Renjin will do it's best to defer operations on large matrices/vectors as long as possible in order to avoid allocating memory for intermediate results, and to apply optimizations like loop fusioning that are only possible if you have a large number of operations to optimize.

So in the following example, you may not get what you expect:

x <- hugeExternalMatrixFromSomewhere()
y <- x      # y *2 contains now only a reference to x, nothing has been computed
changeBackingStoreOfXtoSomethingCompletelyDifferent() # changes y as well !

Maybe we can talk in the new year about your use cases to see how best to get Renjin to do what you would expect. I guess we could have some sort of "volatile" property of a vector that would prevent lazy evaluation and limit access to a vector to a single thread...? But then of course none of the optimizers will kick in....

Happy new year!

